Made a provider's scope a list of permissions rather than one string. It was a `TextLine` holding the space-delimited form OAuth 2 puts on the wire, so the encoding and the value were the same field: a trailing space or a comma typed between two permissions became a scope of its own, and the provider then refused the whole request as an unknown scope. It is a `Tuple` now, rendered as a token widget, and the join happens where the authorize URL is built.

**Stored providers lose their scope and must be re-saved.** The record type changed and there are no upgrade steps, the package being unreleased. A driver's `default_scope` is a tuple as well — a driver declaring a string will now offer a scope per character. Reading is tolerant where storing is not: a value that is still a string is sent as-is rather than re-split, since guessing where one permission ends would only invent tokens.

A `list` setting is coerced to a tuple on the way in, which is what its record accepts. JSON has one sequence type and it decodes to a Python list, so without this every scope submitted through the control panel would have been refused at write time — by a `WrongType` naming the value rather than the shape, well away from the request that caused it.

The demo sites were re-stated to match: both GenericSetup profiles carry the scope as a `Tuple` record with a `TextLine` value type and one `<element>` per permission, and the relying party's setup handler builds its provider with a tuple. It built one with a string, which would have been refused by the record it was written to — the demo install failing outright rather than degrading. @ericof
